# tools/probes — scripts that reproduce a finding

Each script prints the evidence for one confirmed defect that is still open. They are
tools, not tests: `make test` does not run them, and a probe that stops printing its
evidence has stopped finding the defect (move it to a test then).

| Script | Finding | Evidence it prints |
|---|---|---|

Closed since, each probe now a suite:
- a good a person holds read as a skill they have (`held-good-skill.scm`) — a skill is a
  worked spec (formulas/actions `labour-specs`); `tests/planner/test-held-good-is-not-a-skill.scm`.
- free carries of `#:movable` goods admitting a circulation (`movable-circulation.scm`) — a
  carry costs its τ·km in every labour model and opposing carries net before rows are
  emitted (engine `carry-terms`, `net-carries`); `tests/planner/test-property-enact.scm`
  plans movable worlds.
- the claimable set holding metabolic by-products, nutrient pools and a held skill
  (`claimable-by-products.sh`) — the set is read off the scope's own recipes and its
  stocked goods, labour excluded (scope/live `with-claims`); `tests/planner/test-claim-means.scm`.
  Still open there: `claimable-specs` reads the raw rail table and so bypasses the bar
  list `spec-rail` honours (facts/rail.scm, another session's in-flight change).

A fourth finding needs no script: `standing-settings` reads `admit?` and `proc-frag` off a
standing the production reader (`scope-standing`) never carries —

    grep -rn "(cons 'admit?\|'admit? \.\|(admit? \. " module server --include=*.scm   # the test reader only

Read again on 2026-10-09: the two keys are the `standing` reader's interface, exercised by
`tests/planner/test-means-gateway.scm`'s reader (a consensus barring a scope's recipes), so
they stay; the reader contract in `doors/service.scm` now names them.

The sweep that found the calls which could never run is `tools/compile-warnings.sh`
(`make lint-compile`, part of `make test`). The laws that held are
`tests/planner/test-property-{plan,enact,credit}.scm`. The account of all of it, with
causes and the order to close the open ones, is `docs/planning/books/correctness-hunt-2026-10-08.md`.
